{
  "version": "v1",
  "description": "Acknowledged publishes from a dialer to an acceptor. `auth`: the dialer's auth frame with `caps.acks` must carry its own stream id (`stream.id`); without one the session is refused with the code given. `negotiate`: a link carries pubacks when it is a stream link, this side speaks acks (`mine` >= 1) and the peer advertised `caps.acks` >= 1. `puback` / `puback_encode`: the puback control frame; a malformed one is a protocol error of the kind given. `dialer`: one outbound address over a ring log of `capacity` entries with the outbox on. Ops: `publish` appends an event (named by its topic; `acked` waits for its acknowledgement), `connect` brings a link up (`acks`: whether it negotiated pubacks) and sends every entry after the cursor, `disconnect` takes it down, `puback` applies a frame from the acceptor, `fail` resolves every wait with a code. While connected, a publish is sent at once. A puback is cumulative: it reports its own offset and acknowledges every earlier frame not reported by its own puback. An acks link rewinds the cursor to the last acknowledged offset; a link without acks fails every wait with `unsupported`. `sent` lists `topic@offset` in send order (a rewound frame appears again); `resolved` lists outcomes in order (`code` empty: acknowledged); `error` is the protocol error kind that ended the case. `acceptor`: the acceptor's memory of dialer streams under `limits` (`streams` overall, `streams_per_peer` per peer identity: a verified principal, or for a token without one the overall bound, `errors` failed results kept per stream). Each `recv` is an event frame from `peer` on its `stream`; a new frame is published and settles with `result` (`ok`, a code, or `pending` until a `settle` step); a frame at or below the highest offset seen on that stream is a copy, never published again, answered with the first copy's result. A peer that opens too many streams forgets its own least recently used one; past `streams`, the least recently used stream overall is forgotten. A forgotten stream's frames are new again. Past `errors`, the oldest failed result is pruned and a copy at or below it is answered `err`/`unknown`, never `ok`. `expect` is the answer: `publish`, and `status` (`ok`, `err` or `pending`) with `code`.",
